panded to a different  operation  which now i was uh in control of three  different departments  so picking uh counting and also uh  um floor health so floor health was also  a part of the department as well  okay so many people in my audience have  probably seen  the articles that talk about the  conditions for the pickers and they  include things like  um you're not allowed to go to the  bathroom and there's this constant  pressure to pick more and more and more  and the conditions are bad and your  brakes are monitored down to the minute  talk a little bit about what what if  that was true  when you were there is any of it not  true  no it's 100 true amazon is ran  completely off of metrics and numbers  uh they calculate everything it is it's  actually calculated down to the second  you know the seconds you stop scanning  the item uh  your time accum accumulates a time off  task  so um i was a part of that machine i  very much had to monitor  my associates in my department but um  like i said i was just a supervisor that  related to them  so they respected the fact that i wasn't  that type of supervisor that would be  hard on them  if they came back late from break or  whatever the case may be because i know  uh how massive these buildings are and  how the working conditions can take a  toll on your body  now were you pressured even though you  weren't pressuring the pickers maybe the  way that you would be expected to did  that cause a problem for you  absolutely i mean um uh for the most  part  it caused a problem with my own personal  career growth because obviously  management  um knew that i would never side with  them when it came to certain things like  that  um you know i was kind of like the black  people of the uh supervisors  everybody knew that i had a backbone and  um i always spoke up for what was right  and i always sided with the employees at  entry level because that's where i  started from i never forgot where i came  from  and it didn't bother me but it  definitely hindered my career there  you know i applied for a salary manager  position there 49 times and i  i was denied every last one of them and  it's probably because the fact that they  knew  that i would never be fully uh pro  amazon  were you what reason were you given the  49 times why you weren't going to be  given the salary manager  well from my own personal experiences it  was  it was basically bogus um a lot of the  interviews were  set up for me to fail from the beginning  a lot of them the interviews they  already knew who they wanted in those  positions  uh before i even applied so it was a  combination of a lot of different things  um also there was uh you know letters  and  and management above me that didn't like  me as a person you know whatever the  case may be  so it was a lot of different things of  why um i didn't get those positions and  i believe it's systemic you know  systemic racism that  exists inside amazon as well over a  million and a half employees worldwide  and only and then 26 years of existence  only eight percent of the management is  black or brown  so that just tells you right there the  higher up the food chain the less likely  you know you are to be colored so  um i even believe this for the first  time this  past year they have one black woman  that's on the senior team  you know so that just tells you right  there the chances of me getting promoted  up  the ladder was slim and chris i don't  i'm not i'm not familiar with the  numbers you're citing so i'm curious  when you say eight percent  is that globally or in the united states  that's globally  that's globally okay do you know what it  is in the united states  i would guess it would be a lot less  than that you know i'm pretty sure  um it's not too many of us up at the top  you know once again  in this this company's 26 or 27 years of  existence  for the first time ever they put one  black woman on that the senior team  uh the s team they call it um so that  just tells you right there they're way  behind eight ball when it comes to  diversity  uh when it comes to management were  there ways in which  uh working at amazon was better than  when you did  actual customer facing retail like  walmart and target  of course you know i worked at  competitors and i worked  during the holiday season um and you  know  nothing's worse than working black  friday right at the time  you got customers trying to get their  items and and  the lines are backed up and everything's  chaos  chaos  so uh working in the warehouse um you  have to deal with customers  it's a benefit but um once again it's  also calisthenics you know so i used to  tell  my my employees new hires we have a gym  membership you might want to cancel it  [Laughter]  now um ultimately you you were fired by  amazon can you talk about why  yeah absolutely you know last year uh  the beginning of the pandemic  uh late february early march when this  virus  came into the country in seattle uh  killing the senior citizens over there i  i was afraid  over here in the east coast and i tried  to raise health and safety concerns  going through the proper channels  and after doing that for a few weeks i i  tried  organizing workers inside the warehouse  sitting in the cafeteria 10 hours a day  off the clock  i'm telling many as many workers as i  can after i learned in a meeting  with my bosses that uh we had cases in  the facility and they didn't want me to  tell  anybody not to cause a panic or disrupt  business um so i felt that i had to take  further action and after a whole week  of advocating i'm pleading and going  back and forth with the general manager  of the building  um they've decided to quarantine just me  other than everybody else and that  didn't make sense to me  not even the person i ride to work with  none of the people in my department that  was around the person who was infected  it was just me so i knew they were using  me as a target  and as a result i held the protest on  march 30th  outside of my facility and then two  hours later i was terminated over the  phone  a week after that uh jeff bezos and his  top general counsel  david sapolsky they tried to run a smear  campaign on me calling me not smart  articulate  and make me the whole face of the whole  unionizing efforts  so there's a couple different things  here right i mean the one is  that the part about infections at the  facility  and the other is trying to organize uh  the the the workers so let's talk about  the first one first  so you were told by by managers  not to talk about the fact that there  were infected employees  absolutely yes on march 24th we had an  uh  our daily sync meeting which we have  every single day and that's pretty much  giving us the rundown for the rest of  the day  and how it's supposed to go but on that  day it was different  uh that day they told us we had a case  that was actually two weeks prior  uh for the first time and they pretty  much told  the supervisors not to say anything and  i just felt that was  uh wrong i felt like i had to say  something that took my stance there that  was the last day i worked for amazon  so that was a supervisor's meeting and  you guys were told not to mention the  case to the employees you supervise  absolutely okay and then separately  but around the same time you were  working to organize the workers there  and what was the point of the  organization to approach management  about virus-related matters or to  collectively bargain on salary and  benefits  well actually that was the first time i  learned about it  i haven't even started organizing yet  that was march 24th  what i was doing was behind the scenes i  was sending out emails to the governor  to governor cuomo's office to the cdc  department  of manhattan media i was trying to fight  behind the scenes uh  it wasn't until after that meeting the  next day when i came back to the  facility  um i told my supervisor i won't be  working for the remain of the week  and that's when i started to go in the  cafeteria and tell all the workers that  i  came in contact with the truth that you  possibly been  working around somebody who tested  positive um from that point forward  what we did every morning uh a small  group of us  marched into the general manager's  office and voice our concerns  yeah uh has this thing called uh voice  of associates  where associates are allowed to write on  the board uh their complaints  so i said you know what here is your  your voice over  associates board uh right here in person  uh here's our complaints we wanted the  building to just be simply closed down  and sanitized we would have came back to  work no problem and that's all we wanted  in the beginning but obviously a lot  more  now what was officially given to you as  the reason why you were being let go  oh they claimed that i violated socially  this is policies that  none of us ever read are ever you know  implemented you know  as a supervisor i'm supposed to receive  the policies and relay  that information that i never did so  they didn't exist because  they didn't know what we were dealing  with you know and that's all  um it was okay to understand that they  didn't know what they were dealing with  how to  handle it but uh to ignore the fact that  workers  with underlying health conditions you  know mothers that were pregnant  uh parents that have children at home  including myself  were afraid to come to work and refuse  to listen to us that's a whole nother  story  when it comes to what you're fighting  for now if i understand correctly  you're looking to organize uh sort of a  non-union nationwide group of workers am  i right that you're going the non-union  approach and what made you decide to go  about it that way  um yeah you know i i feel like with  amazon there's such a large  entity in a large business that uh  you know it actually would be kind of  difficult for one union to try to  unionize the entire nation uh we're  talking 800 000 workers  so i felt like an organization that has  a worker  driven committee would be more  beneficial for amazon workers uh  something that's working employee  controlled uh but union-like structure  you know i feel like that's the best way  possible it's sort of like cutting down  the middleman  uh no you know no union duels or  whatever the case may be  but workers are allowed to sit in on  negotiation negotiate their own  contracts  and then now we all we also have enough  power  um workers power that if congress said  you know you're an essential worker  and you have to go to work in the middle  of a pandemic right we're going to say  no  we're not going to work until we have  you know  a ppe provided by the company hazard pay  medical leave all these things that  we're demanding  before we consider ourselves essential  workers so that's interesting  so absent the action the the actual  union with the dues  sort of like at what level would you see  this working would it be at the  individual facility level would be at a  regional level or how do you how would  you structure that yeah you know i  thought about it and i think uh  regional would be better um if you had  just one  you know you break down the country into  regions you have a committee  a workplace committee that represents  that region  because we know the cost of living in  different parts of the country is  different  these committees negotiate the contract  and there you go  uh that be able to you know take care of  the mass of the employees that work for  amazon  with unionizing you have to do a  facility by facility  and that's going to be a longer process  as you can see what they're doing in  alabama  they're prolonging it the union busting  yeah um they're firing workers they're  hiring workers  they're doing all these things to  disrupt it uh but if you have a  workplace committee that's something  that's built internally  uh it's gonna be hard to uh bust that up  did you ever see or experience  um or become aware of sort of like not  union busting but maybe union prevention  activity by amazon absolutely  as a supervisor you know i watched uh  you know those training videos we had to  take tests assessment tests  they try to mix in uh certain questions  that  obviously related to union busting but  um i never applied them you know like i  said i was  i'm always uh on the employee entry  level position side so  i never applied these things you know  obviously there was management that  though  that you know applied that and they  still do  um but you know you just have to  understand what you're dealing with  you're dealing with a company that does  that's anti-union  from the beginning and now we see that  it's very much  necessary that they are unionized yeah i  remember  in my early days at circuit city they  sat me down and mixed in with training  videos about like how to use the sales  systems  and what the process is for requesting  time off they throw in videos about  you know we want to maintain a direct  relationship with employees we don't  want any intermediaries or middlemen and  it was very  it was very sophisticated in fact the  way that it was done  oh yeah yeah the same thing you know uh  they mixing  the questions with like safety and  hazmat and all this training and i'm  like well  you know you read between the lines  you're right yeah  uh we've been speaking with chris smalls  former amazon employee now a leader of  the e-commerce labor rights movement  chris i really appreciate your time and  we're going to continue following your  great work  absolutely i appreciate you guys for  giving me a chance to tell my story

